  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\n   We address the Erds minimum overlap problem by optimizing a step function h(x) defined on [0,2] with integral 1. The goal is to minimize C5, which is the maximum correlation overlap between h(x) and 1-h(x) over all shifts k.\n\n   The core idea is to use a combination of gradient-based optimization and pattern search to navigate the non-convex landscape. This hybrid approach allows us to:\n\n   - Explore the neighborhood structure effectively using coordinate transformations\n   - Handle both inequality and equality constraints from 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and \u222bh(x)dx=1\n   - Detect and escape local optima through crossover operations\n\n   The key limitation is the combinatorial search space of grid-based solutions. However, using analytical gradients and efficient constraint handling, we can push toward better solutions progressively.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\n   a. Representation: h(x) is represented as a vector h of length n_points in [0,1]^d with element constraints.\n\n   b. Objective: Minimize f(h) = max_k \u222b h(x) * (1-h(x+k)) dx\n\n   c. Constraints:\n        \u2022 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n        \u2022 \u2211 h_i dx = 1, where dx = 2/n_points\n\n   d. Derived Formulas:\n        Total samples N = n_points\n        Required grid sum S = N/2\n        Probability constant \u03c0 = 2 (for integral scaling)\n\n   e. Optimization Method:\n        - Use SLSQP for local refinement with analytical gradient\n        - Use pattern search for global exploration\n        - Handle constraint with equality: \u2211 h_i = S \n\n   f. Gradient Calculation:\n        \u2207f(h) = d/dh \u2211_{k} [dx * \u2211_{i} h_i * (1 - h_{i+k}) ]\n        For a given k,  \u2207_{h_i} = dx [ (1 - h_{i+k}) - h_{i-k} ] for indices with valid neighbors\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\n   a. Initialization:\n      i. Use FFT interpolation to transfer parent solution\n      ii. Linear scaling for integral enforcement\n      iii. Generate random perturbation for diversity\n\n   b. Local Optimization:\n      i. Compute gradient at current point\n      ii. Perform line search using stateful SLSQP\n      iii. Update solution toward M-best neighbors\n\n   c. Global Exploration:\n      i. Crossover: Parametrized recombination of features\n      ii. Mutation: Exponential smoothing with random walk\n      iii. Thoroughness balance: Dynamic choice between exploration vs. exploitation\n\n   d. Feasibility Maintenance:\n      i. Boundary intersection: Clipping values to [0,1]\n      ii. Constraint restoration: Projection method using scaling factors\n\n   e. Solution Transition:\n      i. Interpolation: Non-uniform grid scaling between resolutions\n      ii. Multiples n: Use doubling method (n, 2*n, 4*n,...)\n      iii. Best-of-n-points: Select optimal solutions via score comparison\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n\n   a. Core Parameterization:\n\n      - Crossover Fraction (0.2-0.3): Typical union makes 70-80% overlap\n      - Mutation Rate (0.1-0.2): Small-window, sliding uniform\n      - Local Steps (3-5): Medoid inspired rounding for erratic decisions\n      - Boundary Strategy: Tridirectional bounce for stuck convergence\n\n   b. Optimization Dynamics:\n\n      - Exploration/Exploitation Cycle:\n        ```mermaid\n        graph LR\n        A[Sensor] --> B(Perceive Local Landscape)\n        B --> C{Is Diverse?}\n        C --> |Yes| D[Pattern Crossover]\n        C --> |No | E[Gradient Descent]\n        D --> F[Global Sampling]\n        E --> F\n        F --> G[Metabolic Check]\n        G --> C\n        ```\n\n      - Active Modes:\n          \u2022 Harvest Mode: Strengthen convergence when stagnation detected\n          \u2022 Explore Mode: Boost diversity when degradation observed\n\n   c. Computational Scheduling:\n\n      - Resolution Transition: Current n = n0 * (2**(t/T))\n      - Dimension M: Variable teaching dimension for secondary experiment\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n   a. Time-Resolution Matrix:\n\n   | Points n   | Operations | Wall Time Estimate |\n   |------------|------------|--------------------|\n   | 45         | 2.06e4     | 2 min              |\n   | 90         | 8.1e5      | 30 min             |\n   | 180        | 3.24e7     | ~2.4h              |\n\n   b. Scheduling Rules:\n\n   - Early Termination: If no improvement in 10 iterations after resolution failure\n   - Timeout Management: 3% budget for constraint failures\n   - Work Distribution: 60/40 split between exploitation, exploration\n\n   c. Failure Modes:\n\n   - Undefined gradients: Handle via finite differences\n   - Invalid collisions: Repair via nearest neighbor projection\n   - Dimension overflow: Resume with half-points grid\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n   a. Final Checks For h:\n\n   - Boundary assurance: For all i, 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1.0\n   - Integral enforcement: (2/n_points)*sum(h) \u2248 1\n   - Score stability: |last_score - prev_score| \u2264 1e-6\n   - Correlation bounds: f(h) < current_best (within tolerance)\n\n   b. Final Execution Sequence:\n\n   ```mermaid\n   sequenceDiagram\n   participant User\n   participant Optimizer\n   User ->> Optimizer: prepare_working_space(n_param)\n   Optimizer ->> Optimizer: init(n_param)\n   loop Iteration cycle\n     Optimizer ->> Fitter: calculate_gradients()\n     Fitter-->>Optimizer: gradient + score\n     Optimizer ->> Navigator: decide_exploration_level()\n     Navigator-->>Optimizer: mode_decision\n     if (harvest) then\n       Optimizer->>Sampler: generate_local_set()\n     else\n       Optimizer->>Sampler: generate_random_set()\n     end\n     Sampler-->>Optimizer: candidate_list\n     Optimizer->>Evaluator: pick_best_by_SLSQP()\n     Evaluator-->>Optimizer: improved_solution\n   end\n   ```\n\n   c. Return Contract:\n\n   - Format: (improved_h, improved_score)\n   - Conditions: Meet all constraints with quantified improvement\n   - Tolerance: Score improvement > 1e-3 relative to current record",
